# Architecture layers

Four layers. Each knows only the layer directly below it.

| Layer | Folder | May import | Must never import |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pages / components | `pages/`, `components/`, `features/*/components/` | hooks, components | `executeGraphQL`, `fetchProcessor`, `.graphql` files |
| Hooks | `hooks/`, `features/*/hooks/` | services | JSX, component files |
| Services | `api/`, `features/*/api/` | clients, queries, generated types | React, `useState`, `useEffect` |
| Queries | `**/query/*.graphql` | nothing | — |

## Rules

- A page or component **never** imports `executeGraphQL` or `fetchProcessor`.
  If it does, a layer has been skipped.
- A service **never** imports from `react`. It has no state and no loading flags.
- A hook **never** returns JSX. It returns an object.
- Unwrap transport shapes in the **service**, as low as possible. Everything
  above deals in clean data.

## Why

When the Apex transport changed from bare `fetch` to the SDK's `fetch` (for
CSRF), only `api/apexClient.ts` changed. No hook, page or component was
touched. That is the layering earning its cost.
